
  [1mThe fork:[0m what the user sees when something they watch changes.

  - [1mBell + dropdown panel[0m — an icon with an unread count; open it for a list of what changed, click an item to jump to
    the task. Buys: the canonical pattern, gives notifications a history and a read/unread state, and the unread badge
    is the visible payoff when you switch users. Costs: the most UI of the four, though still modest.
  - [1mToasts only[0m — transient pop-ups on arrival. Buys: the least code. Costs: no history — miss one and it's gone. And
    with the "acting as" switcher, notifications effectively arrive in a batch the moment you switch, so a stack of
    toasts firing at once is the wrong shape.
  - [1mDedicated inbox view[0m — a separate notifications page. Buys: room for grouping and filtering. Costs: a
    routing/view-switching concept this page doesn't have yet, for a list that will hold maybe a dozen items.
  - [1mBrowser Notification API[0m — real OS-level desktop notifications. Buys: genuinely striking in a demo. Costs: a
    permission prompt, doesn't render if permission is denied, and it's odd paired with the switcher (an OS toast for
    Bob while you're acting as Alice).

  All of these are views over the same stored notification list, so [1mswapping or adding one later is cheap[0m — the
  exception is the Notification API, which drags in permission state.

  [1mMy recommendation: bell + dropdown panel.[0m Given the switcher is the event source, the unread badge changing as you
  switch users [3mis[0m the demo — that's the moment the concept lands. Toasts are the weakest fit here for exactly that
  reason, and the OS-level version is a satisfying add-on once the store exists, not the foundation.
